        ],
        "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14274617/observations/step-q333861-21fa4395b94fbab87369.html",
        "screenshot": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14274617/observations/step-q333861-21fa4395b94fbab87369.png",
        "worked_solution": "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=g(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}Δy={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}=z+Δz.$\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ and $z$ at $x=3,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=3-1=2.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in a table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =xz+y \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1+2 \\\\  & =1+2 \\\\  & =3 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{x+z}{(x-y)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+1}{(1-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |  | $1$ | $2$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $6$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+2=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+6=8 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+4=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $6$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| $3$ | $8$ |  |  | $5$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(3)≈8$\nand\n$z(3)≈5.$",
        "can_dont_know": false,
        "can_skip": false,
        "question": {
          "dom_id": "step-q333861",
          "name": "Question 5",
          "problem": "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x):$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=xz+y,\\, & y(1)=2 \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{x+z}{(x-y)^{2}},\\, & z(1)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y(3)$ and $z(3).$\n\n$\\,y(3)≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,z(3)≈$\n{{field-2}}",
          "worked_solution": "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=g(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}Δy={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}=z+Δz.$\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ and $z$ at $x=3,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=3-1=2.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in a table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =xz+y \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1+2 \\\\  & =1+2 \\\\  & =3 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{x+z}{(x-y)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+1}{(1-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |  | $1$ | $2$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $6$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+2=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+6=8 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+4=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $6$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| $3$ | $8$ |  |  | $5$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(3)≈8$\nand\n$z(3)≈5.$",
          "calculator_instructions": "",
