      "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14274617/observations/step-e21655-a29bfcefb4813451c276.html",
      "screenshot": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14274617/observations/step-e21655-a29bfcefb4813451c276.png",
      "worked_solution": "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=g(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}Δy={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y$ and $z_\\text{new} = z + \\Delta z.$\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ and $z$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 1 - 0 = 1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in a table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $3$ |  |  | $4$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both $y'$ and $z'$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x+5y-z \\\\  & =4\\cdot 0+5\\cdot 3-4 \\\\  & =0+15-4 \\\\  & =11 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =5x-4y+2z \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0-4\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =0-12+8 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $3$ | $11$ |  | $4$ | $-4$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y$ and $\\Delta z$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =11\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =11 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-4)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $3$ | $11$ | $11$ | $4$ | $-4$ | $-4$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $\\Delta x$ to $x,$ $\\Delta y$ to $y,$ and $\\Delta z$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=3+11=14 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=4+(-4)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $3$ | $11$ | $11$ | $4$ | $-4$ | $-4$ |\n| $1$ | $14$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(1) \\approx 14$\nand\n$z(1) \\approx 0.$",
      "can_dont_know": false,
      "can_skip": false,
      "question": {
        "dom_id": "step-e21655",
        "name": "Example: Approximating the Solution to a System of ODEs Using Euler's Method With One Step",
        "problem": "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y = y(x), z = z(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=4x+5y-z,\\, & y(0)=3 \\\\ {z}^{′}=5x-4y+2z,\\, & z(0)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y(1)$ and $z(1).$",
        "worked_solution": "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=g(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}Δy={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y$ and $z_\\text{new} = z + \\Delta z.$\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ and $z$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 1 - 0 = 1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in a table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $3$ |  |  | $4$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both $y'$ and $z'$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x+5y-z \\\\  & =4\\cdot 0+5\\cdot 3-4 \\\\  & =0+15-4 \\\\  & =11 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =5x-4y+2z \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0-4\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =0-12+8 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $3$ | $11$ |  | $4$ | $-4$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y$ and $\\Delta z$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =11\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =11 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-4)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $3$ | $11$ | $11$ | $4$ | $-4$ | $-4$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $\\Delta x$ to $x,$ $\\Delta y$ to $y,$ and $\\Delta z$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=3+11=14 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=4+(-4)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $3$ | $11$ | $11$ | $4$ | $-4$ | $-4$ |\n| $1$ | $14$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(1) \\approx 14$\nand\n$z(1) \\approx 0.$",
        "calculator_instructions": "",
        "fields": [],
        "problem_field_keys": [],
        "answer_fields_complete": null,
        "result": null,
        "proof_feedback": null,
        "html": "<div id=\"step-e21655\" class=\"step\" style=\"display: block;\">\n    \n                 \n                <div class=\"example\">\n                    <div class=\"stepHeader\">\n                        <div class=\"stepName\"><span class=\"stepType\">Example:</span> Approximating the Solution to a System of ODEs Using Euler's Method With One Step</div>       \n                        <div class=\"helpButton\" stepid=\"e21655\">?</div>\n                    </div>\n            \n                    <div class=\"exampleQuestion\">\n                        <style type=\"text/css\">\n                            .mjpage .MJX-monospace {\n                            font-family: monospace\n                            }\n\n                            .mjpage .MJX-sans-serif {\n                            font-family: sans-serif\n                            }\n\n                            .mjpage {\n                            display: inline;\n                            font-style: normal;\n                            font-weight: normal;\n                            line-height: normal;\n                            font-size: 100%;\n                            font-size-adjust: none;\n                            text-indent: 0;\n                            text-align: left;\n                            text-transform: none;\n                            letter-spacing: normal;\n                            word-spacing: normal;\n                            word-wrap: normal;\n                            white-space: nowrap;\n                            float: none;\n                            direction: ltr;\n                            max-width: none;\n                            max-height: none;\n                            min-width: 0;\n                            min-height: 0;\n                            border: 0;\n                            padding: 0;\n                            margin: 0\n                            }\n\n                            .mjpage * {\n                            transition: none;\n                            -webkit-transition: none;\n                            -moz-transition: none;\n                            -ms-transition: none;\n                            -o-transition: none\n                            }\n\n                            .mjx-svg-href {\n                            fill: blue;\n                            stroke: blue\n                            }\n\n                            .MathJax_SVG_LineBox {\n                            display: table!important\n                            }\n\n                            .MathJax_SVG_LineBox span {\n                            display: table-cell!important;\n                            width: 10000em!important;\n                            min-width: 0;\n                            max-width: none;\n                            padding: 0;\n                            border: 0;\n                            margin: 0\n                            }\n\n                            .mjpage__block {\n                            text-align: center;\n                            margin: 1em 0em;\n                            position: relative;\n                            display: block!important;\n                            text-indent: 0;\n                            max-width: none;\n                            max-height: none;\n                            min-width: 0;\n                            min-height: 0;\n                            width: 100%\n                            }</style><p>Consider the following initial value problem for the functions <span class=\"mjpage\"><svg xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\" width=\"18.643ex\" height=\"2.843ex\" style=\"vertical-align: -0.838ex;\" viewBox=\"0 -863.1 8026.8 1223.9\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" aria-labelledby=\"MathJax-SVG-1-Title\">\n<title id=\"MathJax-SVG-1-Title\">y = y(x), z = z(x){:}</title>\n<