      ],
      "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14274617/observations/step-q333831-83ab0700121866220db2.html",
      "screenshot": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14274617/observations/step-q333831-83ab0700121866220db2.png",
      "worked_solution": "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=g(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}Δy={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}=z+Δz.$\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(0)=5$ and $z(0)=7,$ we place $y=5$ and $z=7$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $5$ |  |  | $7$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3x+2y-z \\\\  & =3\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 5-7 \\\\  & =0+10-7 \\\\  & =3 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =x-y+4z \\\\  & =0-5+4\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =-5+28 \\\\  & =23\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $5$ | $3$ |  | $7$ | $23$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method and $Δx=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =23\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =23\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $5$ | $3$ | $3$ | $7$ | $23$ | $23$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=5+3=8 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=7+23=30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $5$ | $3$ | $3$ | $7$ | $23$ | $23$ |\n| $1$ | $8$ |  |  | $30$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(1)≈8$\nand\n$z(1)≈30.$",
      "can_dont_know": false,
      "can_skip": false,
      "question": {
        "dom_id": "step-q333831",
        "name": "Question 3",
        "problem": "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x):$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=3x+2y-z,\\, & y(0)=5 \\\\ {z}^{′}=x-y+4z,\\, & z(0)=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method.\nWhat entries should be placed in the columns for $Δy$ and $Δz$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=1?$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |\n\nTherefore, $y(1)≈$\n{{field-3}}\nand $z(1)≈$\n{{field-4}}.",
        "worked_solution": "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=g(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}Δy={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}=z+Δz.$\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(0)=5$ and $z(0)=7,$ we place $y=5$ and $z=7$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $5$ |  |  | $7$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3x+2y-z \\\\  & =3\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 5-7 \\\\  & =0+10-7 \\\\  & =3 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =x-y+4z \\\\  & =0-5+4\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =-5+28 \\\\  & =23\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $5$ | $3$ |  | $7$ | $23$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method and $Δx=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =23\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =23\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $5$ | $3$ | $3$ | $7$ | $23$ | $23$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=5+3=8 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=7+23=30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $5$ | $3$ | $3$ | $7$ | $23$ | $23$ |\n| $1$ | $8$ |  |  | $30$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(1)≈8$\nand\n$z(1)≈30.$",
        "calculator_instructions": "",
