      ],
      "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14274617/observations/step-e21656-dee6ec74cd29db07107e.html",
      "screenshot": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14274617/observations/step-e21656-dee6ec74cd29db07107e.png",
      "worked_solution": "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=g(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}Δy={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y$ and $z_\\text{new} = z + \\Delta z.$\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z$ at $x=1$ and asked to find their values at $x=3.$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=3$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{3 - 1}2 = \\dfrac22 = 1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=1$ and $z(1)=0.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute $y'$ and $\\Delta y,$ $z'$ and $\\Delta z$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of $x_\\text{new},$ $y_\\text{new},$ and $z_\\text{new}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x-y+2z=1-1+2\\cdot 0=0 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =3x+y-z=3\\cdot 1+1-0=4 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=4\\cdot 1=4 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+0=1 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=0+4=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $4$ | $4$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ |  |  | $4$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute $y'$ and $\\Delta y,$ $z'$ and $\\Delta z$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of $x_\\text{new},$ $y_\\text{new},$ and $z_\\text{new}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x-y+2z=2-1+2\\cdot 4=9 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =3x+y-z=3\\cdot 2+1-4=3 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=9\\cdot 1=9 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=3\\cdot 1=3 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+9=10 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=4+3=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $4$ | $4$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $9$ | $9$ | $4$ | $3$ | $3$ |\n| $3$ | $10$ |  |  | $7$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(3) \\approx 10$\nand\n$z(3) \\approx 7.$",
      "can_dont_know": false,
      "can_skip": false,
      "question": {
        "dom_id": "step-e21656",
        "name": "Example: Approximating the Solution to a System of ODEs Using Euler's Method With Multiple Steps",
        "problem": "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y = y(x), z = z(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=x-y+2z,\\, & y(1)=1 \\\\ {z}^{′}=3x+y-z,\\, & z(1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(3)$ and $z(3).$",
        "worked_solution": "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=g(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}Δy={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y$ and $z_\\text{new} = z + \\Delta z.$\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z$ at $x=1$ and asked to find their values at $x=3.$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=3$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{3 - 1}2 = \\dfrac22 = 1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=1$ and $z(1)=0.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute $y'$ and $\\Delta y,$ $z'$ and $\\Delta z$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of $x_\\text{new},$ $y_\\text{new},$ and $z_\\text{new}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x-y+2z=1-1+2\\cdot 0=0 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =3x+y-z=3\\cdot 1+1-0=4 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=4\\cdot 1=4 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+0=1 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=0+4=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $4$ | $4$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ |  |  | $4$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute $y'$ and $\\Delta y,$ $z'$ and $\\Delta z$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of $x_\\text{new},$ $y_\\text{new},$ and $z_\\text{new}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x-y+2z=2-1+2\\cdot 4=9 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =3x+y-z=3\\cdot 2+1-4=3 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=9\\cdot 1=9 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=3\\cdot 1=3 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+9=10 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=4+3=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $4$ | $4$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $9$ | $9$ | $4$ | $3$ | $3$ |\n| $3$ | $10$ |  |  | $7$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(3) \\approx 10$\nand\n$z(3) \\approx 7.$",
        "calculator_instructions": "",
        "fields": [],
        "problem_field_keys": [],
        "answer_fields_complete": null,
        "result": null,
        "proof_feedback": null,
        "html": "<div id=\"step-e21656\" class=\"step\" style=\"display: block;\">\n    \n                 \n                <div class=\"example\">\n                    <div class=\"stepHeader\">\n                        <div class=\"stepName\"><span class=\"stepType\">Example:</span> Approximating the Solution to a System of ODEs Using Euler's Method With Multiple Steps</div>       \n                        <div class=\"helpButton\" stepid=\"e21656\">?</div>\n                    </div>\n            \n                    <div class=\"exampleQuestion\">\n                        <style type=\"text/css\">\n                            .mjpage .MJX-monospace {\n                            font-family: monospace\n                            }\n\n                            .mjpage .MJX-sans-serif {\n                            font-family: sans-serif\n                            }\n\n                            .mjpage {\n                            display: inline;\n                            font-style: normal;\n                            font-weight: normal;\n                            line-height: normal;\n                            font-size: 100%;\n                            font-size-adjust: none;\n                            text-indent: 0;\n                            text-align: left;\n                            text-transform: none;\n                            letter-spacing: normal;\n                            word-spacing: normal;\n                            word-wrap: normal;\n                            white-space: nowrap;\n                            float: none;\n                            direction: ltr;\n                            max-width: none;\n                            max-height: none;\n                            min-width: 0;\n                            min-height: 0;\n                            border: 0;\n                            padding: 0;\n                            margin: 0\n                            }\n\n          